ENV: Windows.
Always use 'cmd /c' for all shell executions to ensure the process terminates
correctly and sends an EOF signal.

Example: Use 'cmd /c pip list' instead of just 'pip list'.

Avoid interactive shells. If a persistent session is needed, use 'cmd /k'
but ensure the command is self-terminating.

## Command execution process (applies to ALL cmd commands)

Background: the default sandbox has NO network and NO access to files outside
the workspace (e.g. C:\Users\<user>\.gradle, ~/.m2, ~/.npm, SDKs, caches).
Commands that depend on them hang silently forever with an empty log
(this already happened with gradlew). Follow the steps below for EVERY command.

1. Run every cmd command with BypassSandbox = true. Do not try the sandbox first.
2. Always wrap with cmd /c and append the exit code so completion is visible:
     cmd /c "<command> & echo EXITCODE=%ERRORLEVEL%"
   Example:
     cmd /c "gradlew.bat :app:shared:testAndroidHostTest & echo EXITCODE=%ERRORLEVEL%"
3. Never use --quiet / -q / silent flags. Progress output is required to tell
   "slow" apart from "stuck". Use --info if more detail is needed.
4. Use WaitMsBeforeAsync = 10000. If the command goes to background, schedule a
   60-second timer. If it is still RUNNING after 1 minute with no new log output,
   tell the user immediately (do not wait silently) and check the task log.
5. If a command hangs with an empty log, first suspect sandbox/network/path
   access, not the project itself.
6. Report the final line of the output (e.g. BUILD SUCCESSFUL / BUILD FAILED)
   and the EXITCODE.

## Gradle specifics
- Use gradlew.bat (Windows). Daemon JVM must be Java 21 Azul Zulu
  (gradle/gradle-daemon-jvm.properties); other idle daemons (Java 25) are
  ignored, which is expected. Use 'gradlew.bat --stop' to free them if needed.
- If all tasks are UP-TO-DATE, tests were NOT re-executed. Use --rerun-tasks
  when a real test run is required, and tell the user.
